WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.850
Professional cakes: sponges, layers, and finishes that survive the heat.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.320
This is the video for module two of Advanced Baking and Pastry.

3
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:19.440
This module moves you from one-tin cakes to the layered, finished cakes customers pay premium prices for. First, the creaming method.

4
00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:22.400
Then the light whisked sponges.

5
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.550
Then how to stack and coat a cake so it stands straight.

6
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.980
And finally, finishes that hold up in a warm climate.

7
00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:48.680
A Victoria sponge is equal weights of egg, butter, caster sugar and self-raising flour. Weigh the eggs you have, and match them. For example, Amaka's 7 eggs weighed 378 grams, so she used 378 grams of each.

8
00:00:49.930 --> 00:01:02.730
Cream the butter and sugar for about 3 to 5 minutes with a mixer, until pale, almost white and fluffy. You are cutting tiny air pockets that become your rise. This is the step most people rush.

9
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.730
Add beaten egg gradually. All at once and the mixture curdles.

10
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:14.100
If it starts to look grainy and split, beat in a tablespoon of your flour to rescue it.

11
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:23.440
Fold the flour gently, cutting down and turning over, and stop as soon as no dry flour shows. Overmixing makes it tough.

12
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:33.970
Weigh the batter into each tin so layers match, and bake at 180 degrees for roughly 20 to 25 minutes, until a skewer comes out clean.

13
00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:53.070
Whisked sponges rise mainly on egg foam. For genoise, warm the eggs and sugar over simmering water to about 40 to 45 degrees, then whisk for 8 to 10 minutes until a ribbon holds on the surface. It slices into clean thin layers, brushed with syrup.

14
00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:04.780
Chiffon folds an oil and yolk batter into a firm, glossy meringue. Use a grease-free bowl and an ungreased tin, and cool it upside down, or it shrinks and sinks.

15
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:10.200
Because it uses oil, chiffon stays soft in the fridge, which suits fresh cream cakes.

16
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:21.610
For example, Blessing in Abuja is making a three-layer 20 centimetre cake. Never assemble a warm cake. Chill the layers for at least an hour, or overnight.

17
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.770
Cut off the dome with a long serrated knife while turning the cake, and use toothpicks to mark where to split.

18
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:40.050
Brush each layer with simple syrup, equal weights of sugar and water. Blessing uses about 45 millilitres per layer. Too much makes a soggy cake.

19
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:49.850
Pipe a ring of stiff buttercream about 1 centimetre thick around the edge, then spread jam inside it. The dam stops filling oozing out.

20
00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:58.010
Spread a thin crumb coat to seal the crumbs, chill 20 to 30 minutes until firm, then apply the final coat.

21
00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:11.080
A buttercream that is perfect in an air-conditioned room may slide in a hall at 32 degrees. American buttercream is quick and sweet: 1 part butter to 2 parts icing sugar.

22
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:21.380
Swiss meringue buttercream is silky and less sweet. For example, 100 grams of whites, 200 of sugar and 300 of butter.

23
00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:29.240
Ganache is chocolate melted into hot cream. Equal parts dark chocolate and cream makes a soft drip.

24
00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:44.910
Two parts dark chocolate to one of cream sets firm. For an outdoor afternoon event, Ifeoma in Lagos used 600 grams of chocolate to 300 of cream, and her edges stayed sharp much longer than a butter finish.

25
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:57.080
For Swiss meringue buttercream, heat the whites and sugar until they reach 71 degrees on a probe thermometer. This reduces the risk from bacteria such as Salmonella.

26
00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:08.940
Fresh cream, custards, curds, cream cheese icing and fresh fruit fillings are perishable. Keep those cakes refrigerated, and tell the customer to keep them cold.

27
00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:21.810
Do not leave perishable food at room temperature for more than 2 hours, or 1 hour when it is very hot. Wash hands, tools and boards, and keep raw eggs away from finished cakes.

28
00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:31.360
To remember. Use equal weights and cream until pale. Add egg gradually. Choose genoise for structure and chiffon for chilled cakes.

29
00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:38.150
Assemble cold, with a dam and a crumb coat. And pick your finish for the venue's heat, not only the photo.

30
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:48.490
Now bake a weighed Victoria sponge, practise the ribbon stage and meringue peaks, assemble and crumb-coat one cake, and heat test three finishes.

31
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.780
Continue with the lessons below.
